Emacsvox News -- History Of User-Visible Changes

Maintained by Emacsvox Contributors.

* Emacsvox Unreleased --- Current User-Visible Changes

** Speech engine details

Aural Home's /Speech engines/ shows discovered availability and voice counts.
RET opens main and notification discovery, timestamps, runtime diagnostics,
and the separate managed installation. Old inventory is labelled as previous
evidence. Managed checks run asynchronously and retain their results in details;
completion no longer opens an output buffer or speaks its full diagnostic.

** Independent output volumes

Aural Home's /Audio output/ combines notification device selection with five
Omnivox volume controls in Customize:
notification speech, tones and earcons, plus main-stream tones and earcons.
Set each from 0 to 100 percent; 100 preserves its normal level and 0 silences
that component. Changes affect subsequent audio, and saved settings survive
restarts. Independent notification levels require a separate notification
process; notifications using main output share that process's levels.

** Agent Shell response navigation

Upward movement now reaches indented response text that could previously be
skipped with only a blank-line earcon. First response rows are spoken in both
directions, including wrapped text, without leaking hidden prompt markers.

** Simpler feedback controls

Aural Home's /Understand and change feedback/ group now has three entries:
/Feedback for this item/, /Recent feedback/, and /Return to source item/.
Feedback Details combines explanation, playback, and access to changes.
Existing direct shortcuts and searchable action names remain available.
Semantic vocabulary is available through Help, search, and matching criteria.

Feedback Details offers a compact voice choice for the selected field and
access to exact sound overrides, including restoration. Individual voice spans
expand separately, simple field headings are shorter, and unsaved field changes
are marked in the report. Original and simulated playback remain clearly
labelled; previews and retained drafts do not change the captured presentation.

** Names for occasional emoji enabled by default

Occasional emoji are named before engine selection by default.
For example, =Forecast 🔮= becomes =Forecast crystal ball=
for speech, which can avoid an unnecessary engine fallback. The source text
stays unchanged. Naming defaults to at most 64 eligible occurrences per item
and supports buffer-local settings and custom names. Set
=emacsvox-emoji-naming-enabled= to nil to disable it globally or in a buffer.
The higher limit handles decorated mail subjects, including a thinking face
followed by three pizza symbols, without skipping all naming at the third emoji.

Names come from Emacs's full bundled emoji table, including =🌱= (seedling),
flags and keycaps. Arrow and harpoon symbols use bundled emoji names or their
Unicode names, covering all directions, diagonal, double and curved arrows,
including text and emoji presentation selectors.
No per-emoji approval is needed; an explicit restriction
list remains optional. Unknown sequences and other languages
keep their existing fallback behaviour. Speech and voice previews share the
policy, and Feedback Details records substitutions. Original replay preserves
the prepared text after settings change. See /Emoji Speech Names/ in the manual.

** Optional fallback tuning
Choose Customize this voice from an editor fallback row to set individual
adjustments, use the shared value, or restore the adapter default. Automatic
samples audition that row; the same voice draft and Save action own all changes.
Experiment keeps default to the chosen row's voice and adjustments. Shared
changes remain explicit; unrelated draft edits and save recovery are preserved.
Details explains the shared, actual-fallback and contextual sources of the last
confirmed sample's settings.


Released notes are preserved in [[file:NEWS-2026.9.4][NEWS for Emacsvox 2026.9.4]].

** Tracked speech handles early playback replies

Tracked reading now retains playback completion received while a speech request
is still being written. Stopping speech cancels pending callbacks, and failed
submissions cannot restore cancelled tracking or replay the request. Speech
started by playback callbacks gets its own submission.

Stop also cancels speech still being prepared, including a request interrupted
during its automatic stop of older speech. A newer urgent announcement can
supersede that request. Interrupted preparation releases its tracking slots.

** Voice editor gives spoken adjustment feedback

Automatic samples now start with the new adjustment value. Numeric controls
stop at their limits and announce minimum or maximum, and the automatic-sample
toggle announces on or off. Arrow and Tab navigation stop at the first and
last fields without wrapping around the editor.

Complete voice comparisons and individual auditions now share cancellation
handling. Stop and newer previews retire the previous sample's pending work;
late replies cannot start another comparison entry. Individual auditions also
have a playback timeout. A failed or interrupted write is never replayed.

With Omnivox per-choice tuning support, editor previews now preserve individual
fallback settings and contextual overrides. A row can be auditioned or compared
with its original after reordering. Playback feedback distinguishes the sample
from spoken comparison labels and audio that was accepted but never started.
Text changes, undo, save and context recapture retire stale previews. Earlier
playback evidence cannot replace a newer view's result.

** Omnivox 1.10.0 minimum for fallback tuning

Omnivox 1.10.0 or newer is now the supported speech-server baseline for individual
fallback tuning in speech and complete voice previews. The guided Windows and
WSL installers pin 1.10.0, together with matching optional engine modules, and
Debian packages recommend that minimum.

Upgrade an existing Omnivox installation separately. Updating Emacsvox does
not replace your selected server or install optional engine modules.

** Voice palette selections apply consistently

Search and incremental-search hits now use the active palette's bolden voice.
Nonincremental searches, including regexp and history-repeat commands, speak
the destination line and cue a successful hit once.

Completion, status speech, spelling feedback, widgets, and other integrations
now retain named voices instead of freezing legacy ACSS presets. Calendar
marked dates and CPerl/table indentation use the normal speech path, and the
named-voice sampler follows the active palette. Voice Lock and explicit aural
rules retain their existing control over formatting voices.

Restart Emacs after updating to pick up the new voice defaults. Explicit
personal voice assignments are preserved; use names such as ='voice-bolden=
when those assignments should follow the palette. Legacy =voice-*-settings=
remain the fallback for voices without custom palette definitions.

** Responsive shell completion under WSL

Shell command-name completion now skips Windows drive directories by default,
avoiding long pauses scanning directories such as System32. Windows programs
still run by name, and explicit Windows filename completion remains available.
Enable =emacsvox-shell-complete-windows-commands= to restore those command-name
candidates. Native Windows and remote Shell buffers are unchanged.
